RT64 4337374: a triangle with a vertex at or behind the camera got a too-small drawn area (found on THPS2 School II, experiment 59).

RSP::drawIndexedTri estimates each triangle's screen area from its three projected vertices (posScreen = xyz / w). A vertex with
w <= 0 projects to the mirrored side, so the estimate can miss most of the triangle. The estimate feeds the framebuffer pair's
drawColorRect, and the workload renderer makes the pair's viewport as tall as that rect (getTargetsFromPair: nativeColorHeight =
drawColorRect.bottom; FramebufferRenderer::addFramebuffer: viewport height = targetHeight). A pair holding the frame's full-screen
clear never notices. A pair split off mid-frame (rt64-tlut-range's case, or a real read of the image being drawn) clipped a ground
triangle running under the camera at the short edge: flat patches of the clear colour. Fix: take the whole scissor for such a
triangle. Three compares per triangle on the CPU; the area only grows.

--- a/src/hle/rt64_rsp.cpp
+++ b/src/hle/rt64_rsp.cpp
@@ -1142,7 +1142,17 @@
             fbPair.scissorRect.merge(scissorRect);
 
             FixedRect drawRect;
-            for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
+            // [thps-recomp] A vertex at or behind the camera (w <= 0) projects to the mirrored side of the screen, so the three screen
+            // positions don't bound the triangle. Take the whole scissor (clipped to the viewport below): the pair's viewport is as tall
+            // as its drawn area, and a too-short area clipped ground running under the camera (THPS2 School II, frames split in several pairs).
+            const auto &posTransformed = workload.drawData.posTransformed;
+            const bool behindCamera = (posTransformed[globalIndices[0]].w <= 0.0f) || (posTransformed[globalIndices[1]].w <= 0.0f) ||
+                (posTransformed[globalIndices[2]].w <= 0.0f);
+            if (behindCamera) {
+                drawRect = scissorRect;
+            }
+
+            for (int i = 0; (i < 3) && !behindCamera; i++) {
                 const hlslpp::float3 &v = posScreen[globalIndices[i]];
                 drawRect.ulx = std::min(drawRect.ulx, int32_t(v[0] * 4.0f));
                 drawRect.uly = std::min(drawRect.uly, int32_t(v[1] * 4.0f));
